-- title:
--   Igusa: K(j(q),j(q^N)) contains every j(qᵈ), ℓ∤ N
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $\ell$ be a natural number with $\mathrm{CharP}\,K\,\ell$ and $\ell \neq 0$, so that $K$ has positive characteristic $\ell$, and let $N$ be a nonzero natural number with $\ell \nmid N$. Inside the field of formal Laurent series $K((q))$, write $j_K(q)$ for `jqModC K`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the power series obtained from the integral numerator series `jNum` by reducing its coefficients along $\mathbb{Z} \to K$, and for $d \geq 1$ write $j_K(q^d)$ for the image of $j_K(q)$ under the substitution $q \mapsto q^d$ (`qExpand K d`). The assertion is an equality of two intermediate fields of $K((q))$ over $K$: the field $K\bigl(j_K(q), j_K(q^N)\bigr)$ generated by the two elements `jqModC K` and `jqNModC K N`, and the field generated by the whole set `divisorExpansionsC K N` of all substitutions $j_K(q^d)$ with $d$ a nonzero divisor of $N$. Thus adjoining the single expansion at level $N$ already produces all the intermediate expansions.
--
--   This is the positive-characteristic half of Igusa's theorem on the reduction of the modular equation: for $\ell \nmid N$ the level-$N$ modular function field in characteristic $\ell$ is generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ alone, which corresponds to the irreducibility of $\Phi_N$ modulo $\ell$ and to geometric irreducibility of the special fibre of $X_0(N)$. It is used downstream in the analysis of places and level structures on the Čerednik–Drinfel'd side and in the theory of mod $p$ modular forms.
